[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "ADR-2608190400"
  :adr/title "Being switched on is not being at work"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-19"
  :adr/body
  "## Context

`workforce-status` answered `enabled 70`, and that number was true and useless
twice on the same day.

It stayed 70 through the eighteen hours in which one held run occupied the
single slot and the fleet ran nothing at all (ADR-2608190100). It stayed 70
through the day on which most ticks that did run failed on a plan step no tool
could satisfy (ADR-2608190200) -- 326 of 461 stored runs. Whatever was wrong,
the surface that exists to report on the workforce reported the same number.

ADR-2608190200 made a no-op recordable as `:safe-no-op` and then said, in its
own last paragraph, that nothing counted it. This is that.

## Decision

`workforce-status` carries `:outcomes` -- what the last
`resident-outcome-window` (50) resident runs came to, with the window's own
bounds beside it.

**`:completed` and `:no-op` are both successes and are counted apart.** A Bot
that finds nothing to do on every tick and a Bot that does work are the same
colour to `:agent.run/status`, and telling them apart is the entire reason the
`:safe-no-op` result exists. One number for both would have made the fleet
look fine on a day it did nothing.

**Failures keep the provider's own name**, and keep it through the wire.
`:provider/timeout` is a slow model, `:provider/unreachable` is a network,
`:internal-error` is a fault in this application. They have different fixes,
and merging them is precisely what made an afternoon of timeouts look like a
bug here (ADR-2608190300).

The counts are keyed by the FULL name as a string, because the first version
was not. `json/write-str` renders a namespaced keyword as its name alone, so
`:provider/timeout` reached every JSON reader of this surface as `timeout` --
indistinguishable from another namespace's timeout, or from a bare one. It was
noticed by reading the live tool output an hour after this landed: the ADR
above said failures keep their name, over a surface that dropped it.

**No runs is reported in words, not as an empty tally.** `:outcomes` is `nil`
with a note when this owner has no resident run recorded. `{}` reads as
\"nothing wrong\" and means \"nothing measured\"; that substitution is the
failure this workspace keeps finding, and an instrument built to catch it must
not commit it.

**A window, not all of history**, because the question is what the workforce is
doing now, and a store running for weeks would drown a change in its own past.

**Interactive Goals are not counted.** A person's own Goal succeeding is not
the workforce being productive.

## What was NOT done, and why

The plan was to shrink the resident prompt, on the reasoning that 3.3k prompt
tokens per request at 75.8 tokens/second was 44 seconds of a 120 second wall.
Measuring it first retired the idea: the system prompt is **707 tokens** and
the tool definitions **782**, about 1.5k static, and it carries policy rather
than padding. Observed completions ran 990 tokens across 8 turns -- roughly
124 each, about 11 seconds, nowhere near the 768 ceiling. A typical request is
therefore around 55 seconds against a 120 second wall, which is what the
measured median of 35 seconds says too.

The timeouts are the tail, not the norm. Changing a live system's prompt on an
argument that its own numbers do not support would have been a change made for
the feeling of having done something.

## Verification

Four tests. Two were reverted to confirm they discriminate:

  letting an empty tally print as a healthy one
    -> an-owner-with-no-resident-run-is-told-so-in-words,
       an-interactive-goal-is-not-counted-as-workforce-activity
  folding `:no-op` back into ordinary success
    -> the-status-counts-doing-nothing-apart-from-doing-work

## Still not settled

The instrument says what happened; it does not say whether a Bot reporting a
safe no-op on every tick has nothing to do or is being asked the wrong
question. That is about the objectives in `network-awai/loop-yakuwari`, and it
is now answerable with a number instead of an argument."}]
